"Combines terrain elevation and recognizable surface features to explain landforms and support geographic orientation.\n\nUse when: Understand slopes, ridges, valleys and surrounding features. Provide terrain context for planning or field activity.\n\nAvoid when: The source scale or age cannot support the precision expected for the task.\n\nVisual grammar: Elevation contours and labeled index lines describe terrain relief. Symbols distinguish water, transport, vegetation and built features. Scale, coordinate reference and vertical datum support interpretation.\n\nBuild: Choose a suitable map extent, scale and elevation source. Combine terrain with selected current reference features. State contour interval, horizontal reference and elevation convention.\n\nCheck: Contours and elevation labels use consistent units and datum. Surface features match the required date and scale. Preserve terrain and route topology; separate crowded layers for legibility.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming all maps labeled topographic have the same contour interval. Treating a generalized map as an exact engineering survey.\n\nExample: A park planning view shows the ridge, drainage valleys, paths and facilities, helping explain why a proposed trail alignment climbs steeply.\n\nAlternatives: Reference map, Isoline map."
